In Python you rank strategies with `sorted(strats, key=lambda s: s.sharpe, reverse=True)`. C++ passes a rule instead: a function that says whether `a` goes before `b`.

```cpp
std::sort(strats.begin(), strats.end(),
    [](const Strat& a, const Strat& b) {
        return a.sharpe > b.sharpe;
    });
```

A lambda can use a local variable if its **capture list** names it. `[limit]` copies `limit` into the lambda. `std::count_if` counts the elements for which the lambda returns true.

```cpp
double limit = 0.02;
auto big = std::count_if(rets.begin(), rets.end(),
    [limit](double r) { return std::abs(r) > limit; });
```

